This is a really interesting design space. Let me think through what's actually broken with the things you're combining, and then work toward something that could genuinely pull people in.
What's wrong with current AI chat

It's solitary. You ask, you get an answer, and you have no idea if that answer is any good. There's no accountability, no way for the answer to improve after it's given, and no serendipity — you only ever see responses to your own questions. It's useful but forgettable.
What's wrong with current communities

Reddit's signal-to-noise ratio is getting worse. Good answers take hours or days to surface. The same questions get asked repeatedly. TikTok-style feeds are engaging but ephemeral — nothing accumulates into lasting knowledge. Discord communities have great energy but information drowns in chat scroll.
The interesting gap

The space between "instant but lonely AI" and "social but slow communities" is mostly unexplored. The question is: what happens when an AI response becomes the starting point for human discourse rather than the endpoint?

Here's where my thinking goes:

The core mechanic: AI answers are drafts, not answers.

Someone asks a question. AI responds immediately — but that response is explicitly framed as a first draft, a seed. It's published into a categorised feed where it's immediately open to the community. The thread then evolves:

    Someone validates it ("confirmed, I work in this field")
    Someone challenges part of it ("this is misleading because...")
    Someone adds lived experience ("I tried this, here's what actually happened")
    Someone forks it ("interesting, but what about this angle instead?")

Then — and this is the part that could make it genuinely different — the AI synthesises the human input and produces an improved version. The thread visually shows the evolution: original AI response → human contributions → refined response. The AI isn't a tool that fires and forgets. It's a visible participant that acknowledges corrections, asks follow-ups, and learns from the thread.

Why this could work socially:

One dynamic I keep coming back to is making correction rewarding rather than adversarial. On Reddit, correcting someone starts a fight. Here, correcting the AI is a valued contribution — "AI was wrong" moments become celebrated rather than confrontational. People aren't attacking each other, they're collectively improving a draft. The ego dynamics are completely different when the thing being corrected is a machine.

The feed / discovery layer:

This is where the TikTok instinct comes in. You don't just see your own questions. You see a feed of interesting questions and evolving answers from across the community. Some you'd never have thought to ask. You can swipe through, react quickly ("AI nailed this" / "AI missed the point"), or dive deep. Trending threads, active debates, recently-improved answers — all surfaceable.

The "beat the AI" angle:

What if there's an explicit mechanic where humans can submit competing answers? The community votes on whether the AI or the human answer is better. This creates genuine engagement — it's a game with stakes. Over time, people who consistently improve on AI in certain domains earn visible domain reputation. Not karma, not points — demonstrated competence in specific areas.

AI as synthesiser in debates:

When humans disagree with each other in a thread, the AI steps in to map the disagreement fairly — summarising both positions, identifying where the real contention lies, flagging where people are actually agreeing but using different language. This is something current communities desperately lack and that AI can do well.
Where this connects to your vertical site-building

Each domain you build could have one of these communities embedded in it, tuned to the vertical. A veterinary site has an AI-assisted community where pet owners ask questions, AI gives initial guidance, and actual vets refine those answers. The vet builds reputation. The knowledge accumulates. The site becomes genuinely valuable rather than just another content site.
What I think needs more thought

A few tensions I'm not sure how to resolve yet:

Moderation at scale. If AI responses are public and humans can amend them, you get the wiki vandalism problem. But you also have AI as a potential moderator, which is an advantage traditional platforms don't have.

The cold start problem. Communities need a critical mass to feel alive. If someone asks a question and no humans engage, it's just a worse AI chat. How do you make the AI-only experience good enough to retain people until the community forms?

The identity of the AI. Is it a neutral tool, or does it have a voice and personality? A neutral tool is safer but less engaging. A personality creates attachment but risks people anthropomorphising it in unhelpful ways.

Revenue / sustainability. Does domain expertise become monetisable? Could verified experts offer paid consultations surfaced through the platform?

