Now there's a conversation. A one-on-one with an AI that's pushing back on your opinion. That's already more engaging than most AI chat experiences because it's adversarial in a playful way. It's not trying to help you. It's trying to challenge you.

And everything you produce — your response, your counter-response, the exchange — is being stored. When the next person arrives ten minutes later, they don't see an empty room. They see your exchange. They can react to it, respond, remix it. The first person was the community seed, and they didn't even need to know that's what they were doing.
