Moderation — keeping "provocative" from becoming "toxic"

This is the tension at the heart of Spark. The mechanic is literally provocation. You're asking people for hot takes, roasts, adversarial positions. That's one wrong turn from becoming 4chan.

But I think the room/arena structure actually gives you moderation tools that feed-based platforms don't have.
The AI referee has real power

In a room, the AI isn't just narrating — it's running the game. And a game master can enforce rules without it feeling like censorship. When a referee in football gives a yellow card, nobody screams about free speech. It's just the rules of the game. Spark can establish the same social contract:

The AI can in real time:

    Flag responses that attack people rather than ideas ("That's a foul — play the ball, not the person. Rephrase?")
    Escalate a response from "visible" to "under review" without deleting it — the response dims, gets a "flagged" badge, community can vote on whether it crosses the line
    Adjust room temperature — if a challenge is producing more personal attacks than creative responses, the AI can intervene: "Room's getting heated. Reminder: sharpest take wins, not angriest."
    Reward the right behaviour — by highlighting clever, funny, or insightful responses prominently, the AI shapes what "good" looks like without ever saying what "bad" looks like

This is tone-setting through positive selection rather than negative enforcement. You don't tell people what not to do. You show them what gets rewarded. The anonymous person writing something hateful sees it sink without trace while the person with a genuinely sharp take gets the room's attention. The incentive structure does most of the moderation work.
Provocation design as moderation

The biggest lever is actually upstream — in how provocations are framed. A provocation that says "This person is an idiot — agree or disagree?" invites personal attacks. A provocation that says "This decision will look genius or catastrophic in 5 years — which?" invites analysis. The framing engine controls the entire tone of the room.

This means the curation gate at Layer 3 isn't just filtering for engagement — it's filtering for the kind of engagement it produces. Over time, the system learns which framings produce creative responses and which produce bile. You can literally A/B test provocation styles against toxicity rates.
The ephemeral nature helps

Toxic content on Twitter lives forever. Screenshotted, quote-tweeted, amplified. On Spark, a bad response in a challenge that expires in 2 hours has a very short half-life. It doesn't get indexed. It doesn't get shared (only Moments get shared, and those are curated). The architecture itself limits the damage radius of any individual toxic response.
Community immunity

Here's something interesting about the reaction system. If "Cursed" is a reaction option, the community has a vocabulary for calling something out without engaging with it. One-tap "Cursed" on a toxic response, the response accumulates Cursed reactions, it sinks. No argument. No pile-on. No engagement that feeds the troll. Just a collective signal and the response disappears from prominence.
