Examined user journey progression from initial contact through commitment

Let me think about this from the perspective of someone who's never heard of Spark, lands on vonc.com, and what happens over the course of their first days and weeks.
The first visit — the zero-to-one moment

Someone arrives. Maybe they clicked a shared Moment. Maybe a friend sent them a challenge link ("think you can beat my response"). Maybe they just typed vonc.com out of curiosity. They don't know what this is.
The first 5 seconds

The page loads and there's no signup wall, no cookie banner dominating the screen, no "welcome to Spark, here's our mission statement." There's a provocation card. Something happening in the world right now, framed sharply, with a text input beneath it and a ticking timer.

The provocation has to do a lot of work in this moment. It needs to be immediately comprehensible — no jargon, no context required. It needs to be interesting enough that someone pauses. And it needs to feel like it has an obvious entry point — "I could say something about this."

Underneath the main card, visible without scrolling, there's a subtle secondary layer: "The AI's take" — a response the AI generated, clearly labelled. It's decent but beatable. Not terrible (that would make the platform feel low-quality), not brilliant (that would be intimidating). It's the "surely I can do better than this" baseline.
The first 30 seconds — they type something

If the provocation works, they type a response. No account needed. They hit enter. Immediately two things happen:

First, their response appears on screen with a small animation — the "launched" sound plays if they have audio on. There's a visual acknowledgement that their words went somewhere. This matters — the worst feeling on any platform is typing something and feeling like it vanished.

Second, the AI responds. Not instantly — a brief pause, maybe 2-3 seconds, enough to feel like something is thinking. Then a counter-argument, a follow-up question, or a reframe. "Interesting — but what about [angle you didn't consider]?" This isn't a chatbot conversation. It's a sparring move. The tone is respectful but challenging. The goal is to make the person think "wait, actually..." and want to respond again.
The first 2 minutes — they're in a loop

They respond to the AI. The AI pushes back again. Maybe this goes 2-3 exchanges. At this point, something subtle happens — a small notification: "4 other people are responding to this right now." Not usernames, just a number. The room is getting occupants. If they scroll down, they can see other people's responses starting to appear, with reaction buttons beside them.

They haven't signed up. They haven't committed to anything. But they've already created content, had a conversation, and can see other humans engaging with the same provocation. The hooks are in.
The first 5 minutes — the soft ask

After their exchange, the platform surfaces one more thing: a small card showing today's other active provocations (the lobby, in miniature). "3 challenges live right now." And beneath that: "Want to save your responses? Create a free account in 10 seconds."

The account creation is minimal. Email or social login. No profile to fill out. No preferences to set. No onboarding wizard. They can do all of that later. Right now, the only thing the account does is save what they've already done and let them see their responses alongside others.

If they don't create an account, that's fine too. Their response still exists in the room. They can come back later and claim it. The platform doesn't punish anonymous participation — it gently rewards identified participation.
What they leave with

Even if they bounce after 5 minutes, they've had an experience. They've written something, been challenged on it, and seen that other people are doing the same thing. That's more than most platforms offer in a first visit. And if the provocation was good, they're carrying it with them — "actually, what IS the right answer to that question?" is a thought that brings people back.
Day 1-3: The discovery phase

They come back. Maybe they created an account, maybe not. The landing page remembers their previous visit (cookie-based) and shows them something new — today's provocations, plus a note: "Yesterday's challenge closed. Your response got 7 reactions. Want to see how it ranked?"

That's a pull. They click. They see the challenge results — the top responses, the AI's recap, and their own response positioned within the field. Maybe they were 15th out of 40. Not great, not embarrassing. They can see what the top responses did differently. This is learning without being taught.
Finding their mode

Over the first few days, the platform introduces mechanics gradually — not all at once:

Day 1: They've seen Arena (the provocation-response-sparring loop). That's all they need to understand initially.

Day 2: They return and notice a Stage room in the lobby alongside the Arena rooms. It has a different colour, a different energy label. They might enter out of curiosity. The first Stage challenge they encounter feels different — instead of "what's your take?" it's "show us something." If they're a maker type, this clicks immediately. If they're more analytical, they might prefer Arena. Either way, they've now seen both modes.

Day 3: The platform surfaces the Daily Gauntlet — "5 provocations, 5 minutes, discover your Spark type." This is the personality-quiz hook. It's positioned as a game, not a tutorial. But by completing it, they've engaged with both Arena and Stage prompts, received a score, and been given their first archetype label. "You're an emerging Surgeon — precise, analytical, cuts to the core." Now they have an identity on the platform, even if it's provisional. That identity is a hook for return.
What the platform learns

In these first three days, even with minimal explicit interaction, the platform has gathered significant data about the user:

    What kinds of provocations made them stop and engage vs scroll past
    Whether they lean Arena or Stage
    Their response style — long or short, analytical or creative, fast or considered
    Their reaction patterns — do they "Genius" generously or sparingly? Do they "Cursed" often?
    What time of day they visit, how long they stay, what pulls them back

This is the seed of their emergent profile. The platform doesn't tell them any of this yet — it's too early. But it's shaping what the platform shows them next.
Week 1: The habit-forming phase

By the end of the first week, the platform's goal is to establish at least one habitual behaviour — something the user does without being prompted.
The Daily Gauntlet as anchor habit

The strongest candidate for a daily habit is the Gauntlet. It's short (5 minutes), it's scored (progress is visible), and it produces a result (archetype refinement, stats comparison to yesterday). If the user completes 3 Gauntlets in a row, they have a streak. Streaks are psychologically powerful — breaking a streak feels like losing something. Duolingo built a billion-dollar business substantially on this mechanic.

The Gauntlet also gets smarter over the week. Day 1, it's generic provocations. By Day 5, it's tuned — it noticed they engage most with food and fashion topics, so the Gauntlet includes more of those. The scoring gets more nuanced: "Your response speed on fashion prompts is 3x faster than on tech. Your originality peaks on food topics." The user is learning about themselves through the platform.
The first social moments

During week 1, the platform engineers a few specific moments designed to create social connection:

The first real reaction. At some point in the first few days, someone reacts to one of their responses. The platform makes this visible — a notification, a small animation on their response. "Someone reacted 'Genius' to your take." This is the first moment of social validation. It's tiny but it matters disproportionately. The platform might even gently prioritise showing new users' responses to active reactors, increasing the chance this moment happens quickly.

The first "you're not alone" moment. The platform shows them that other people have similar reaction patterns, similar archetype profiles, similar topic preferences. "12 people on Spark have a similar profile to yours." Not a suggestion to connect — just an awareness that their people exist. Curiosity is planted.

The first chain participation. If they respond to a provocation and someone remixes their response, the notification is: "Someone built on your take." They click, they see their response transformed — someone took their idea and pushed it further. This is the IKEA effect amplified — not just "I made something" but "I started something." It's a stronger hook than any reaction because it implies their contribution had creative value.
Discovering depth

By mid-week, casual exploration reveals more mechanics:

    They tap on someone's response and see their emergent profile — and realise they have one too
    They notice the Prediction Arcade and try a prediction for the first time
    They enter a Stage room and see the "Teach Me" reactions, the breakdowns
    They see the Rising section and notice it's showcasing newcomers like them
    They browse The Drift during a commute and realise it's a good way to stay current

None of this is forced. It's all discoverable. The platform reveals itself through use rather than through instruction. Each discovery feels like finding a hidden room in a game — a small reward for curiosity.
Week 2-4: The investment phase

The user has been coming back regularly. They have a streak. They have an archetype. They've participated in maybe 15-20 challenges. Now the platform's job shifts from habit formation to deepening investment.
The profile becomes meaningful

By week 2, their emergent profile has enough data to be interesting:

"Joined 12 days ago. 18 challenges entered (14 Arena, 4 Stage). Archetype: The Surgeon. Strongest domains: food, fashion. Contrarian 68% of the time. Response speed: top 30%. 2 chains started from their responses. Prediction accuracy: 55% (above baseline). The AI says: 'Precise thinker who finds structural flaws others miss. Could take more creative risks.'"

This profile is a conversation piece. It's shareable. It's something they think about. "Am I really that contrarian?" They might deliberately try a non-contrarian take next time to see if the profile shifts. The platform is becoming a lens for self-understanding.
Niche gravity

The platform has identified their interests by now. If they consistently engage with food content, they start seeing niche Stage rooms: "Tonight's food challenge: impress us using only pantry staples." They enter and find 20 other people who care about food as much as they do. They see showcases that inspire them. They see breakdowns that teach them something. Someone reacts "Teach Me" to one of their responses for the first time.

This is the "found by a community" moment. It doesn't feel like joining a group — it feels like realising you were already part of one. The niche becomes a home base. They still explore Arena broadly, but Stage becomes about their niche.
The first "I'm getting better" moment

Their glow-up tracker has enough data points now. The platform surfaces it: "Your first food showcase got 4 reactions. Your most recent got 23. Here's the trajectory." They can see their own improvement. This is the moment that separates casual use from committed use. If someone can see they're getting better at something, they'll keep coming back to maintain that progress. It's the same psychology that makes fitness trackers sticky.
Social bonds forming

By week 3-4, they've seen the same names (post-reveal) in their niche rooms multiple times. They recognise certain styles. They might have been in a crew together. They've remixed each other's responses. A social graph is forming organically — not through following, but through repeated proximity in shared contexts.

The platform could surface this gently: "You and [username] have been in 6 of the same challenges this week. You complement each other — you're analytical, they're creative." This isn't a follow suggestion — it's a collab suggestion. "Want to team up for tomorrow's food Stage?" If they do, and the collab works, they've made their first genuine connection through the platform.
Month 2-3: The commitment phase

The user is now a regular. They have a developed profile, an established niche, recurring collaborators, a prediction track record, and a visible glow-up arc. The platform shifts again — from deepening investment to expanding horizons and offering new challenges.
Cross-niche exploration

The AI starts occasionally suggesting provocations outside their comfort zone: "You dominate food challenges. But tonight there's a design challenge with a similar structure — constraint-based creativity. Want to try?" Cross-pollination. The user discovers they're good at things they hadn't tried. Their archetype evolves. "The Surgeon → The Surgeon-Wildcard. You've been branching out."
The mentorship path

They're now experienced enough that newer users look up to them. Their breakdowns get "Teach Me" reactions. They might get invited to mentor in a niche room. The AI facilitates: "3 newcomers in the food Stage this week are responding to the same themes you explored in your first month. Your breakdown on [topic] could help them." Mentoring is its own reward — it feels good to be asked, and teaching deepens your own understanding.
The recognition plateau

Sometime around month 2-3, the user hits a natural question: "What am I doing this for?" The novelty has worn off. The habits are established. The social connections exist. The platform needs to provide a satisfying answer beyond "keep doing what you're doing."

This is where the deeper mechanics earn their keep:

    Serialised challenges give ongoing narratives to follow — "I predicted this 3 months ago, the reveal is next week"
    Glow-up milestones create celebration moments — "Your 50th showcase. Here's your journey so far"
    New formats keep things fresh — a format they've never tried rotates in
    Competitive seasons — monthly or quarterly resets with cumulative achievements
    The Tier 4 path starts becoming visible — they see that curators and mentors with their level of reputation are being invited to brand collabs, or have opened subscription channels

The platform doesn't push toward monetisation. But it makes the path visible. "Creators at your level in similar niches have earned £X from brand collabs this month." The aspiration is planted.
Month 6+: The established user

By now they have a rich history on the platform. Their profile tells a story. They have a glow-up reel worth sharing. They have a network of collaborators and people who tune in to their showcases. They might be earning from the platform.

But the key thing about this phase is that they'd feel a genuine loss if Spark disappeared. Not because of the money. Because of the identity they've built, the community they've found, the skills they've developed, the archive of their creative journey. This is the deepest form of retention — the platform has become part of how they understand themselves.

This is what Instagram achieved early on (before it became an advertising platform) and what TikTok achieves for its top creators. The difference Spark offers is that this depth of identity isn't reserved for the top 1% of creators. The archetype system, the niche communities, the glow-up tracking, the curator role — these give everyone a meaningful identity, whether they're a daily Drift browser with a sharp taste profile or a Stage regular with 50 showcases and a growing niche following.
The journey as a design principle

The important thing about this timeline is that at no point does the user feel like they're being onboarded, trained, funnelled, or manipulated. Every step feels like their own choice. The platform doesn't say "now try this." It makes things discoverable and lets curiosity do the work.

The sequence is: intrigue → creation → validation → habit → identity → community → mastery → purpose. Each stage builds naturally on the previous one. And at each stage, the user could stop and still feel satisfied. Someone who only ever does the Daily Gauntlet and browses The Drift is having a complete experience. Someone who becomes a top niche creator with a subscription channel is having a different but equally complete experience.

The platform accommodates both without making either feel like they're using it wrong.

Now let me get this into the doc.
