Doc updated with the full user journey from first visit through to established user.

The thing that crystallised for me while writing this is that the journey sequence — intrigue → creation → validation → habit → identity → community → mastery → purpose — is actually a useful design test for every feature. Any new mechanic should serve at least one of those stages. If it doesn't fit anywhere in that sequence, it's probably not needed.

The other insight is about the engineered social moments in Week 1. Those three moments — first reaction, first "you're not alone," first chain participation — are the fragile bridge between "person who tried it once" and "person who comes back." If the platform can reliably deliver those three experiences within someone's first week, the rest of the journey has a much higher chance of playing out. They're worth investing disproportionate engineering effort in — even if it means slightly biasing the system to make them happen faster for new users.

The recognition plateau around months 2-3 is the other danger zone. That's where most platforms lose their second wave — people who aren't going to be professional creators but who've outgrown the novelty. The serialised challenges and competitive seasons are the answer there, but they need to be genuinely good, not just "we reset the leaderboard." The "previously on..." recaps for serialised challenges could be a real retention tool if they're done well — they're essentially "last time on your favourite show" but for real-world predictions you personally made.

