Good question. Let me think through every point where AI gets called and what kind of model it needs, because some of this is surprisingly cheap and some of it could get expensive fast if we're not careful.
The always-running background layer

This is the stuff that happens whether anyone's on the site or not.

Scraping and ingestion — not really AI cost, that's your existing scraping infrastructure doing HTTP requests and parsing. The AI cost starts when scraped content needs to be understood.

Layer 2: Framing engine — this is the big volume generator. Every scraped item gets 5-10 candidate provocations and springboards. If you're scraping, say, 200 interesting items per day, that's 1,000-2,000 framing calls. But these are short prompts with short outputs — "here's a trend, give me 5 challenge framings." A Mistral 7B or Llama 8B on CPU handles this easily. Each call is maybe 200 tokens in, 300 tokens out. At 2,000 calls per day, that's roughly 1 million tokens per day. On your own CPU hardware, the cost is essentially electricity and depreciation — pennies.

Layer 3: Curation gate — reviewing those 1,000-2,000 candidates and picking the best 15-20 for the day's challenges. This needs slightly better judgement. You could do it with a larger local model (Llama 70B on GPU) or keep it human initially. If AI, it's maybe 50-100 calls per day with longer context (here are 10 candidates, rank them). Moderate cost, low volume.

Layer 4: Mashup engine — this is where you need genuine intelligence. Finding non-obvious connections between unrelated items. A foundation model call — Claude or GPT-4 class. But it's maybe 2-5 calls per day. At foundation model pricing, that's a few pence per call. Negligible.

Layer 5: Serialisation tracker — mostly database queries. Occasional summarisation call when it's time for a "previously on..." recap. Maybe 1-5 calls per day. Cheap model is fine.

Layer 6: Niche detector — clustering analysis on user behaviour data. This is more traditional ML/statistics than LLM work. Could be done with embeddings and clustering algorithms rather than chat completions. Low cost.

Background total: roughly 1-2 million tokens per day on cheap local models, plus a handful of foundation model calls. On your own infrastructure, this is probably under £5/day even at scale.
The per-challenge AI cost

Each challenge that goes live has AI involvement:

Room hosting/commentary — the AI narrating room state ("Room's heating up, 20 responses in"). This is templated, not generative. "Response count is X, top reaction is Y, time remaining Z." You could do most of this with simple logic and string templates. Zero AI cost for 80% of it. The occasional "the AI calls out a good response" moment needs a quick evaluation call — "is this response notably better than the average?" That's a small, cheap model call. Maybe 5-10 per challenge. Tiny.

Recap generation — when a challenge closes, AI synthesises all responses into a recap. This is one call per challenge with a longer context (all responses as input, structured summary as output). If you run 15-20 challenges per day, that's 15-20 calls. A Llama 70B on GPU can handle this. Each call might be 2,000-5,000 tokens in, 500 out. Moderate but manageable.

"The Crowd Speaks" synthesis — similar to recaps. One call per challenge. Same cost profile.

Director's Cut replay — more structured than creative. Pulling key moments from the challenge timeline and narrating them. One call per challenge. Could be a cheaper model.

Per-challenge total: roughly 20-30 small model calls per challenge, plus 2-3 medium calls for recaps/synthesis. At 15-20 challenges per day, that's maybe 300-600 small calls and 40-60 medium calls daily.
The per-user AI cost — this is where it gets interesting

AI sparring partner (cold start / solo mode) — this is the most AI-intensive feature per-user. Every exchange is a model call. If someone does 3 exchanges on a provocation, that's 3 calls. If 100 people per day use the sparring partner, that's 300 calls. If 10,000 people per day use it, that's 30,000 calls. This scales linearly with users.

However — these are short exchanges. Maybe 200 tokens in (provocation + user response + brief history), 150 tokens out (counter-argument). A Mistral 7B or Llama 8B handles this well. On your CPU infrastructure, the latency might be 2-3 seconds (which actually works — it feels like the AI is "thinking"). Cost per call is very low on local hardware.

Daily Gauntlet scoring — 5 provocations per user per day. The provocations themselves are pre-generated (free). The scoring needs AI to evaluate originality: "how different is this response from the predicted common response?" That's 5 calls per user per Gauntlet. But the scoring could be done in batch — collect all Gauntlet responses and score them together periodically. Cheaper model is fine.

Archetype generation and profile updates — periodic, not real-time. Run a batch job nightly that updates all active profiles. One call per active user, but it's pattern-matching against behaviour data, not creative generation. Could be done with embeddings and classification rather than LLM calls entirely.

Niche room creation and collab suggestions — these happen at the platform level, not per-user. Clustering runs periodically. When a niche room is created, one call generates the challenge prompt. When a collab is suggested, one call generates the matchmaking explanation. Low volume.

Glow-up reel generation — triggered when milestones are hit. Maybe a few hundred per day across all users. One call each. Short summary of progress. Cheap model.

Mentor breakdown structuring — when a creator posts a breakdown, AI helps structure it. "People want to know about X." One call per breakdown. Low volume — maybe 20-50 per day even at moderate scale.
