Foundation model costs are still negligible because they're only used for mashups and occasional high-intelligence tasks — maybe £5-10/day even at this scale.
Where the cost risks are

AI sparring is the expensive one. It's the best feature for cold start and solo engagement, but it's also the only feature that generates a full LLM call for every single user interaction. Everything else is either batch-processed, per-challenge (not per-user), or can be done with templates and simple logic.

Ways to manage this:

    Rate limit sparring sessions — maybe 3 per provocation per user, then "the AI rests, but check back when others have responded"
    Use the smallest model that maintains quality — sparring doesn't need deep reasoning, it needs quick, relevant pushback. A 3B parameter model might work
    Cache common counter-arguments — if 500 people give similar responses to a provocation, the AI's pushback can be partially pre-generated
    Make sparring a "premium" feature once the community exists — when there are enough humans in rooms, the AI sparring becomes less necessary

Gauntlet scoring at scale is the other scaling concern. But this is very batch-friendly — you don't need real-time scoring. Collect responses, score them in bulk every 15 minutes. Batch processing is much more efficient than individual calls.
The cost-per-user picture

At 100,000 DAU with the architecture above:

    Infrastructure cost (compute, storage, bandwidth): maybe £300-800/day
    Foundation model API costs: £5-10/day
    Total: roughly £300-800/day, or £0.003-0.008 per daily active user

That's 0.3p to 0.8p per user per day. A low subscription of £3-5/month covers the AI costs for a paying user many times over. Even if only 5% of users pay, that's 5,000 paying users × £3 = £15,000/month against maybe £15,000-24,000/month in compute costs. Close to break-even on subscriptions alone, before any advertising or brand challenge revenue.

For comparison, Reddit's cost per DAU is estimated at roughly $0.01-0.02/day, and they're profitable primarily through advertising. Spark's cost structure is in the same ballpark.
What's genuinely cheap

The provocation engine (the entire Layer 1-6 pipeline) is cheap regardless of scale. It produces maybe 15-20 challenges per day whether you have 50 users or 500,000. The per-challenge costs (recaps, synthesis, hosting) scale with challenges, not users. So the content production side of the AI costs is essentially fixed.

The reputation system, archetype tracking, glow-up mechanics, niche detection — these are mostly database operations with occasional light AI calls. They scale with standard infrastructure, not with expensive model inference.

The reaction system, crowd energy visualisation, scoring, leaderboards — no AI at all. Pure application logic.
What to watch

The per-user interactive AI (sparring, Gauntlet real-time scoring) is the cost that could run away. Design it with throttles from the start. And keep measuring whether the sparring partner is actually driving retention — if it is, the cost is justified. If people stop using it once the community exists, it naturally scales down.

